<h1 class="article-title">Public Meeting on the Environmental Impact Assessment for the Planned NorSAF Sustainable Aviation Fuel Plant</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>On the evening of 4 March, an initial public consultation meeting on the environmental impact assessment (EIA) for the planned NorSAF sustainable aviation fuel plant took place, with detailed explanations and discussions. The aim was to inform residents about the proposal and explain the steps to be taken as part of the EIA process. The meeting was held at Liepāja State Technical School and attended by 162 people in person and 51 remotely via Zoom.</p><p>Before the NorSAF sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) project can proceed, an EIA must be completed. In accordance with a programme issued by the State Environmental Service (SES), this will involve detailed studies of the proposed technologies, production processes and, most importantly, their effects on people and the environment.</p><p>Stages of the EIA procedure:</p><ul><li>On 14 January 2026, the SES decided to initiate the EIA procedure;</li><li>On 20 February 2026, the initial public consultation was announced in a newspaper. Information letters were sent to residents whose properties are adjacent to the planned production site;</li><li>On 4 March 2026, the public consultation meeting took place;</li><li>Until 12 March 2026, residents, state authorities and local government institutions were invited to send proposals for the EIA programme to the State Environmental Service by email at pasts@vvd.gov.lv or by post to Rūpniecības Street 23, Riga, LV-1045.</li><li>Within one month, the SES prepares the EIA programme based on the application and the outcome of the initial public consultation.</li><li>Sector experts will assess the technologies, environmental impacts and safety issues in accordance with the programme and prepare the report.</li><li>A public consultation on the completed report will take place in the second half of 2026.</li><li>The EIA procedure will conclude with an opinion from the SES on the planned facility’s effects on people and the environment.</li></ul><p>NorSAF, a company operating in the Liepāja SEZ, is developing a sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) project. It has a strategically important role in reducing CO₂ emissions from European aviation and helping to achieve the European Union’s sustainability and climate-neutrality objectives.</p><p>NorSAF aims to establish a modern, safe and environmentally responsible facility that complies with EU environmental requirements and international safety standards. The project envisages using sustainable feedstocks and supporting aviation’s transition to lower-emission solutions. The company supports the national strategy to move towards an entirely green power grid and plans to use only renewable energy to operate the plant. More information: www.norsaf.com/lv</p><p>About the technologies</p><p>The project is based on PureSAF®, a licensed technology from US company KBR — a unique Alcohol-to-Jet (ATJ-SKA) solution. The technology was developed in cooperation with Swedish Biofuels, which has worked on innovative SAF solutions for more than 20 years. PureSAF® has been tested for several years by the Swedish Armed Forces, DARPA (USA), the US military, Lufthansa and various research commercialisation organisations. A small pilot plant funded by the European Commission has also operated for several years at a technical university campus in Stockholm, Sweden.</p><p>KBR is responsible for scaling and engineering the technology. It is one of the world’s leading engineering companies, with more than a century of experience in industrial projects in the energy, chemicals, infrastructure and defence sectors. For the past 60 years, KBR has also provided technology and engineering solutions for NASA space programmes, demonstrating high standards of quality, safety and innovation.</p><p>More information: www.kbr.com/en</p><p>What does this project mean for Liepāja and the region?</p><ul><li>Around 200 new high-value-added jobs in engineering, chemistry, energy and logistics, plus approximately 200 jobs during construction.</li><li>Development of businesses in the city and region, including the hospitality and leisure sectors.</li><li>Long-term tax revenue for municipal and national budgets.</li><li>Stronger positioning of Liepāja as a climate-neutral city and a centre of industrial innovation in the Baltics.</li><li>A contribution to Latvia’s energy independence and security.</li></ul><p> </p><p>This is a strategically important national energy-security and economic-development project that has already received Green Corridor status. It is aligned with Latvia’s RIS3 development strategy, supports climate objectives, strengthens the regional economy and positions Liepāja as a future centre for green energy and aviation fuel in Northern Europe.</p><p>Additional questions can be sent to: jautajumi@norsaf.com</p></div>
